      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "which we ended up",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/2"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/14",
      "text": "which we ended up"
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": "https://venturebeat.com/2012/12/13/airbnb-acquires-localmind/",
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "selling to Airbnb",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/2"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/15",
      "text": "selling to Airbnb"
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "in 2012. Up until that point I’d been a software engineer my entire professional life, so I had no real idea of what product management was. And looking back, I was really bad at it. I knew very little about managing up, keeping scope in check, leading teams within a larger org, and basically everything else a PM should know. Also, having just run my own company for 1.5 years, it took me waaaaaay to long to remember that you had to actually listen to your boss when they said no. 🥴",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/2"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/16",
      "text": "in 2012. Up until that point I’d been a software engineer my entire professional life, so I had no real idea of what product management was. And looking back, I was really bad at it. I knew very little about managing up, keeping scope in check, leading teams within a larger org, and basically everything else a PM should know. Also, having just run my own company for 1.5 years, it took me waaaaaay to long to remember that you had to actually listen to your boss when they said no. 🥴"
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "If I could go back in time and give myself some advice, here are the top five things I’d tell myself:",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/body"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/17",
      "text": "If I could go back in time and give myself some advice, here are the top five things I’d tell myself:"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "$ref": "#/groups/4"
        }
      ],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "enumerated": true,
      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "list_item",
      "marker": "",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/3"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/18",
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "formatting": {
        "bold": true,
        "italic": false,
        "script": "baseline",
        "strikethrough": false,
        "underline": false
      },
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "Being a PM is very different from being a founder —",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/4"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/19",
      "text": "Being a PM is very different from being a founder —"
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "formatting": null,
      "hyperlink": null,
      "label": "text",
      "meta": null,
      "orig": "Although being a PM is actually much closer to being the CEO of the product than people acknowledge, there are enormous differences between these two roles. One of the most interesting is that as a founder your job is essentially to win at all costs. You are rewarded if your company succeeds — everything else gets swept under the rug. Even if you create constant chaos, repeatedly change course, and piss people off. As a PM, your job is to",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/groups/4"
      },
      "prov": [],
      "self_ref": "#/texts/20",
      "text": "Although being a PM is actually much closer to being the CEO of the product than people acknowledge, there are enormous differences between these two roles. One of the most interesting is that as a founder your job is essentially to win at all costs. You are rewarded if your company succeeds — everything else gets swept under the rug. Even if you create constant chaos, repeatedly change course, and piss people off. As a PM, your job is to"
